title: 'M1.4 slice 3 review: read tokens become proof-of-possession; P8-D89 bump lands with slice 3'
summary: 'Aegis found a bearer read token is harvestable by a same-user relaying impostor (rename the socket, relay hello so serverSig verifies, receive the plaintext token; reusable up to 24 h) and that revocations roll back by deleting a jsonl line. Orchestrator decision inside the spec-silent issuance area: read tokens become Ed25519 proof-of-possession credentials (mint row stores the public key; auth signs nonce‖serverKeyId‖endpointPath‖negotiated), revocation sticky + tamper-detected. Seam hardening T-009(c) merged (78be43ec, a141957a) after reviewer + Aegis round-2 APPROVE. The P8-D89 single clientApiId bump is folded into slice 3 round 2.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#identity'
  - '#socket'
  - '#kernel-log'
  - '#contract-event-v1'
commit: a141957a
decisions:
  - id: P8-D89a
    decision: Read tokens are proof-of-possession (Ed25519 seed held by the client, public key in the root-signed mint row), not bearer secrets; revocations are sticky in the kernel and a shrinking/edited token file fails closed
    rationale: Spec §3.1/§4.9 is silent on read-token issuance; a bearer token defeats serverSig against a relaying impostor and survives in dumps/logs; PoP matches device auth and keeps 'the file holds no secret' true
